  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=(5t-15){e}^{t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment.\n\nThe acceleration is zero at $t=$\n{{field-1}}, and the velocity at this moment is\n$v=$\n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((5t-15){e}^{t}) \\\\  & =5{e}^{t}+(5t-15){e}^{t} \\\\  & =(5t-10){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((5t-10){e}^{t}) \\\\  & =5{e}^{t}+(5t-10){e}^{t} \\\\  & =(5t-5){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5t-5){e}^{t} & =0 \\\\ 5t-5 & =0 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=1.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\nv(1)=(5\\cdot 1-10){e}^{1}=-5e.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration is zero at $t=1,$ and the velocity at this moment is $v=-5e.$",
